<onlyinclude>A '''motherboard''' (or ''mainboard'') is the principal circuit board of a computer that mechanically mounts and electrically interconnects processing, memory, expansion and peripheral subsystems, distributes power and hosts platform-support circuitry.</onlyinclude>

== Functions and boundaries ==
A desktop motherboard commonly provides a processor socket or soldered processor, memory connectors, expansion interfaces, storage and peripheral connectors, voltage-regulation circuitry and firmware storage. Chipsets or integrated processor I/O controllers route selected interfaces; the physical presence of a connector does not establish the number of available lanes or a particular protocol generation. The board does not itself define the instruction set architecture or replace the CPU.

== Platform integration ==
Power-supply connectors and local regulators provide distinct voltage rails. Form factors constrain mounting and connector placement, while compatibility also depends on CPU socket and firmware support, memory generation, electrical signaling and thermal design. A physically compatible expansion card is not necessarily capable of operating at its advertised maximum bandwidth.

